{
  "title": "How to Sleep Better: A Science-Backed Guide for Restful Nights",
  "content": {
    "root": {
      "type": "root",
      "format": "",
      "indent": 0,
      "version": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Are you tired of tossing and turning? Have you tried all the usual tips—counting sheep, warm milk, you name it—and still end up staring at the ceiling at 3 AM? You're not alone. So many of us struggle to get the deep, restful sleep our bodies and minds are craving. This isn't just another list of generic advice. We're going to build a real, science-backed plan to help you finally achieve better sleep.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "ef9532066f1a6fbd51d815c1",
          "type": "upload",
          "value": 916,
          "fields": null,
          "format": "",
          "version": 3,
          "relationTo": "media"
        },
        {
          "type": "quote",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "\"Forget just 'trying' to sleep. It's time to build a system that invites rest naturally, every single night.\"",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 2,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Why a Good Night's Sleep Feels More Elusive Than Ever",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "It seems like a simple thing, right? Lie down, close your eyes, and drift off. But for a huge number of adults, it's a nightly battle. According to the CDC, about 30% of adults are sleep-deprived, meaning they get less than the recommended amount of sleep. This takes a toll on everything: your energy, your mood, and your ability to focus during the day. It's frustrating when you do all the right things and still can't get the rest you need.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "This guide will walk you through everything you need to know about how to get better sleep. We'll cover the core habits, how to set up your environment for success, and ways to calm a racing mind. While lifestyle changes are the foundation, sometimes your body needs a little extra support to find its balance. Combining good habits with natural support can be a powerful way to improve sleep quality.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "This article contains affiliate links. If you purchase a product through one of these links, we may receive a commission.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "For those looking to give their new routine a boost, a supplement like NuviaLab Relax is designed to support relaxation and emotional balance. Its natural ingredients can help calm your system and prepare you for a restful night. You can ",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "fields": {
                "url": "https://nplink.net/ww00kjcb",
                "newTab": false,
                "linkType": "custom"
              },
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 3,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "learn more about how it works here",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": ".",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "The Science of Sleep: Understanding Your Body's Internal Clock",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "To fix our sleep, we first have to understand how it works. Your body has a built-in 24-hour clock called the circadian rhythm. Think of it as your internal master clock that tells you when to be awake and when to feel sleepy. This clock is heavily influenced by light and darkness.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "During the day, light tells your body to be alert. As it gets dark, your brain releases a hormone called melatonin, which is like a signal that it's time to wind down. At the same time, another hormone called cortisol—often known as the stress hormone—should be at its lowest. Modern life, with its bright screens and irregular schedules, can really mess up this natural rhythm.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "As you sleep, your brain cycles through different stages:",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "ul",
          "type": "list",
          "start": 1,
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 1,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Light Sleep:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " This is where you start to drift off. Your muscles relax, and your heart rate slows. It's easy to be woken up during this stage.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 2,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Deep Sleep:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " This is the physically restorative stage. Your body works on repairing tissues, building bone and muscle, and strengthening your immune system.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 3,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "REM Sleep:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " REM stands for Rapid Eye Movement. This is where most of your dreaming happens. It's crucial for your mind, helping you process emotions and lock in memories. If you want to learn more, you can read about ",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "type": "link",
                  "fields": {
                    "url": "https://2ahealthylife.com/blog/en/what-is-rem-sleep",
                    "newTab": false,
                    "linkType": "custom"
                  },
                  "format": "",
                  "indent": 0,
                  "version": 3,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "mode": "normal",
                      "text": "what is REM sleep",
                      "type": "text",
                      "style": "",
                      "detail": 0,
                      "format": 0,
                      "version": 1
                    }
                  ],
                  "direction": null
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " in detail.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            }
          ],
          "listType": "bullet",
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Getting enough of all these stages, especially deep and REM sleep, is key to waking up feeling refreshed and ready for the day.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Pillar 1: Master Your Daily Habits for Better Nights",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Great sleep doesn't start when your head hits the pillow. It starts with the choices you make all day long. Creating consistent, healthy habits is the most powerful way to regulate your body's internal clock and pave the way for restful nights. These are some of the most important ",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "fields": {
                "url": "https://2ahealthylife.com/blog/en/sleep-hygiene-tips",
                "newTab": false,
                "linkType": "custom"
              },
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 3,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "sleep hygiene tips",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": " you can implement.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Stick to a Consistent Schedule",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "This might be the most important tip of all. Try to go to bed and wake up at the same time every day—yes, even on weekends. This consistency reinforces your body's circadian rhythm, helping it know when to release sleep and wake hormones. It might be tough at first, but your body will thank you for it.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Get Smart About Light",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Light is the number one signal for your internal clock. Try to get some bright sunlight first thing in the morning. A 15-minute walk outside can work wonders to tell your body, \"Hey, it's time to be awake!\" In the evening, do the opposite. Dim the lights in your home an hour or two before bed to signal that it's time to wind down.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Watch What You Eat and Drink",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "tag": "ul",
          "type": "list",
          "start": 1,
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 1,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Caffeine:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " We all love our coffee, but caffeine has a long life in your system. Experts recommend stopping all caffeine intake at least 6 to 8 hours before your planned bedtime to prevent it from interfering with your sleep.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 2,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Alcohol:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " A glass of wine might make you feel sleepy initially, but it actually disrupts your sleep later in the night. Studies show alcohol suppresses REM sleep, which is essential for mental restoration.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 3,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Large Meals:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " Avoid eating a heavy meal right before bed. Digestion can be a very active process that can keep you from falling into a deep sleep. A light snack is okay if you're hungry.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            }
          ],
          "listType": "bullet",
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Pillar 2: Engineer the Perfect Sleep Environment",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Your bedroom should be a sanctuary for sleep. Optimizing your environment sends a powerful message to your brain that this space is for rest and nothing else. Think of it as creating the perfect cave: cool, dark, and quiet.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "e736b20456c69c74964e8cc6",
          "type": "upload",
          "value": 917,
          "fields": null,
          "format": "",
          "version": 3,
          "relationTo": "media"
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Make It Dark",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Even tiny amounts of light from a phone charger or a streetlamp can disrupt melatonin production. The goal is total darkness. Consider investing in blackout curtains or a comfortable sleep mask. Cover or remove any electronics that emit light. Every little bit helps.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Keep It Cool",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Have you ever noticed it's harder to sleep when it's hot? That's because your body temperature naturally needs to drop to initiate sleep. The National Sleep Foundation suggests the ideal bedroom temperature is between 60-67°F (15-19°C). So, turn down that thermostat before you climb into bed.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Keep It Quiet",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Unexpected noises can easily pull you out of light sleep. If you live in a noisy area, earplugs can be a simple and effective solution. Some people also find that a white noise machine or a fan helps by creating a consistent, soothing sound that masks other disruptive noises.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h2",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Pillar 3: Calm Your Mind with a Wind-Down Ritual",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "You can have the perfect habits and the perfect bedroom, but if your mind is racing with worries, sleep will be hard to find. A relaxing wind-down routine is your secret weapon against pre-sleep anxiety. It signals to your brain and body that the day is over and it's time to switch off.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "30c3ef261e40efeb35d2d9c0",
          "type": "upload",
          "value": 918,
          "fields": null,
          "format": "",
          "version": 3,
          "relationTo": "media"
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Implement a 'Digital Sunset'",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "The blue light emitted from phones, tablets, and computers is especially disruptive to melatonin production. Make a rule to put all screens away at least 60-90 minutes before bed. This is a non-negotiable for better sleep habits.",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "h3",
          "type": "heading",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Try Relaxation Techniques",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "mode": "normal",
              "text": "Instead of scrolling, use that last hour to do something genuinely calming. Here are a few ideas:",
              "type": "text",
              "style": "",
              "detail": 0,
              "format": 0,
              "version": 1
            }
          ],
          "direction": null,
          "textStyle": "",
          "textFormat": 0
        },
        {
          "tag": "ul",
          "type": "list",
          "start": 1,
          "format": "",
          "indent": 0,
          "version": 1,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 1,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Deep Breathing:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " Inhale slowly for four counts, hold for four, and exhale for six. Repeat this a few times to slow your heart rate.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 2,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Journaling:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " If your mind is full of worries or to-do lists, get them out of your head and onto paper.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
              ],
              "direction": null
            },
            {
              "type": "listitem",
              "value": 3,
              "format": "",
              "indent": 0,
              "version": 1,
              "children": [
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": "Reading:",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 1,
                  "version": 1
                },
                {
                  "mode": "normal",
                  "text": " A physical book (not on a screen!) is a fantastic way to escape and relax.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "style": "",
                  "detail": 0,
                  "format": 0,
                  "version": 1
                }
